  "headline": "Money supply outpacing production growth fuels inflation in Bangladesh",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "A World Bank report states that money supply outpacing production growth in Bangladesh is fueling inflation, as government borrowing creates a liquidity surge while private-sector credit flow slows amid production deceleration. According to the report, broad money growth, encapsulated as M2, accelerated from 6.5 per cent in June 2025 to 12.4 per cent in May 2026, while inflation remained elevated through FY2025-26. The development financier predicted that the link between broad money and inflation may be strengthening, as monetary expansion increasingly coincides with persistent price pressures. The report suggests that monetary expansion may be contributing more directly to price pressures than in previous years, when the relationship between money-supply growth and inflation was described as less pronounced. The World Bank noted that growth in M2 has been driven by increases in both net domestic assets (NDA) and net foreign assets (NFA). Previously, moderation in money-supply growth was partly driven by substantial foreign-exchange sales by Bangladesh Bank (BB), but recent developments indicate a shift in the relationship between liquidity and prices."
    }
  ]
}
